  <div id="sutta">
  
<h2 class="h1">Thoroughly Confused<br/>
Susammuṭṭha Sutta  (SN 1:8)</h2>
<p><em>Like the similar sutta <a href="SN1_7.html" class="sutta-ref">SN 1:7</a>, this sutta offers evidence in support of the translation, “awakened” for the Pali term,</em> Buddha. <em>The verses draw a direct connection between two words derived from the same root: the adjective</em> sambuddha, <em>a variant of</em> buddha, <em>and the verb</em> pābujjhati, <em>which in context clearly means to awaken from sleep.</em></p>
<p>Standing to one side, a devatā addressed the Blessed One with a verse:</p>
<div class="verse">
<pre>“Those for whom
phenomena are thoroughly confused,
who may be led
by the teachings of others:
	Asleep are they;
	they haven’t awakened.
It’s time for them
to awaken.”</pre>
<p class="vspk">The Buddha:</p>
<pre>“Those for whom
phenomena are not confused,
may not be led
by the teachings of others.
	Awakened
	through right knowing,
they go among the discordant
harmoniously.”<span class="fn"><a href="#sn1.8note01">1</a></span><!--end fn cite--></pre>
</div><!--end verse-->
              <div class="note">
                <p class="notetitle">Note</p><!--end notetitle-->
<p id="sn1.8note01">1. See <a href="SN1_7.html#sn1.7note01" class="sutta-ref">note 1 under SN 1:7</a>.</p>
              </div><!--end notes-->
<p class="seealso">See also: <a href="../MN/MN41.html" class="sutta-ref">MN 41</a>; <a href="../MN/MN61.html" class="sutta-ref">MN 61</a>; <a href="../MN/MN97.html" class="sutta-ref">MN 97</a>; <a href="../AN/AN6_55.html" class="sutta-ref">AN 6:55</a>; <a href="../AN/AN6_63.html" class="sutta-ref">AN 6:63</a></p>
      </div><!--end:sutta-->
